# Compiled WASM binaries. THE HEADING IS NOW TRUE OF THE TREE: nothing here is tracked.
#
# It was not true from 2026-08-01 to 2026-09-10. Six substrate modules were force-added —
# dla-canonical-{wat,llvm,emcc,rust,asc,zig}.wasm — and the paragraph that used to sit here
# recorded the trade they were accepted under:
#
#     "`bytelock.yml` installs only wabt, lua5.4 and Go; it has no clang-wasm32, emcc,
#      rustc+wasm32, asc or zig. Those five substrates therefore CANNOT be rebuilt on the
#      runner, and a substrate CI cannot build is a substrate CI would silently SKIP. …
#      Add a toolchain to the workflow and the corresponding file should stop being
#      tracked, not start being trusted."
#
# THE TOOLCHAINS WERE ADDED, so the files stopped being tracked. Run 34490525095 measured
# every one of them on all five byte-lock legs: wabt, clang+lld, `rustup target add wasm32`,
# assemblyscript, zig 0.13.0 and emscripten install in 52-75 seconds total on Linux and
# macOS, all six substrates build, and each rebuilt module reproduces the UNCHANGED
# `testdata/golden-seed-*.json` vectors. Two per-leg gaps are upstream facts rather than
# oversights and are recorded in `bytelock.yml`'s matrix: Chocolatey's `wabt` package ships
# no `wat2wasm`, so Windows gets wabt from its official release archive; and emsdk publishes
# no Windows-arm64 SDK at all ("tool or SDK not found: sdk-releases-…-64bit"), so
# Emscripten is honestly TOOLING-ABSENT on `windows-11-arm` and that leg's floor says so.
#
# The gap between this file's intent and the tree cost two weeks (2026-08-15):
# `dla-canonical-zig.wasm` was committed as an `ar` archive and nobody re-derived it,
# because everyone read this heading and assumed it was built fresh. If you ever commit a
# substrate here again, `git add -f` it AND record why it is tracked rather than built —
# and expect `hygiene/audit-proof-lineage-binaries.ts` to hold you to the five conditions
# in `.claude/rules/no-binary-in-proof-lineage.md` rather than to this comment.
#
# `*.rcgu.o` is rustc's per-codegen-unit intermediate. One
# (`dla_canonical_rust.…-cgu.0.rcgu.o`) was committed by accident and sat here unreferenced
# until the audit above named it; the pattern below keeps the next one out.
*.wasm
*.wasm.o
*.o
*.a
*.rcgu.o
wasm_exec.js
!wasm_exec.js
# The Pages build copies the JS bridge out of the $GOROOT that compiled the module, so it
# is a build output under a distinct name — the committed `wasm_exec.js` beside it is a
# Go 1.22-era file and MEASURED 2026-08-17 to differ from Go 1.26.4's. Two names keeps
# that difference visible instead of letting one overwrite the other.
wasm-exec-pages.js
go.sum

# Build artifacts
zig-cache/
zig-out/
.zig-cache/
